From a43a0770035d928b267e6fc69c67033163e833d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nineap <trkir21@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:41:55 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(core): reverse egress + reliable Connect delivery

Why
---
1. A browser (or any client) opening dozens of connections at once lost
   some of them for good: `Connect` frames went through `try_send` into a
   leg's 16-slot control queue, a full queue dropped the frame with only a
   rate-limited WARN, and since a Connect is never retried and carries no
   ack, the local socket hung forever. Measured with the new
   `connect_burst` tool (64 simultaneous streams x 3 rounds, local echo
   target): old client 63/192 echoed, 129 hung; with this change 192/192,
   0 hung. Connect (all variants) is now a critical frame like
   Close/Heartbeat/Credit: `send().await`, which stalls only the per-stream
   task that issued it.

2. Nodes behind NAT (a home PC, a router) had no way to be an exit. With
   reverse egress the node dials OUT to an ingress (token
   `egress:<label>:<client token>`) and serves as the exit for flows of that
   ingress's own clients. Data-plane roles follow `StreamHandler::opener`,
   not who dialed: the dialer's handler opens targets, the acceptor keeps
   `opener == None` for that session and opens streams towards the dialer
   through a `MeshPeerSession` view (same bridge as mesh hops,
   `bridge_via_peer`).

What
----
* `ServerHandler::with_reverse_egress(registry)` enables the role on an
  ingress; without a registry `egress:` tokens are refused (before, they
  were treated as ordinary tokens).
* `AuthValidator::validate_egress` - default-deny. An ordinary client token
  must not grant the exit role (anyone could otherwise capture other
  clients' traffic); it needs `require_auth` and a validator.
* `ExitMode` (Local default / Prefer / Require) and per-client `ExitPolicy`
  (Default / Local / Egress(label)). `Require` and a missing labelled
  egress fail closed: the flow is closed, never exited from the ingress.
  UDP flows are closed instead of leaking out locally while a remote exit
  is wanted.
* `EgressPolicy` on the dialing side: loopback/private/link-local/CGNAT/
  metadata targets refused by default (`allow_private` opts in), the
  checked address is the one connected to (no second resolution, DNS
  rebinding cannot swap it), `max_streams` caps concurrent flows (extras
  get Close, the slot is held until the flow ends).
* The dialer is TCP-only (no datagram leg).
* `tools/loadtest`: `connect_burst` binary (public client API only, builds
  against the old core too, for before/after comparison).
* docs/REVERSE_EGRESS.md.

Compatibility
-------------
No wire changes: no new frame types, same handshake. Cross-version matrix
(real `netrunner-server` process + `connect_burst`, 64 streams x 3 rounds):
  old client / old server:   63 echoed, 129 hung
  old client / new server:   80 echoed, 112 hung  (works, old client bug)
  new client / old server:  192 echoed,   0 hung
  new client / new server:  192 echoed,   0 hung
A server without a registry behaves as before except for refusing
`egress:` tokens. An old server would treat such a dialer as an ordinary
client session that never receives flows (harmless no-op).

Tests
-----
cargo test --workspace: all green (netrunner-core 301 passed).
cargo test -p netrunner-core --features "pcap mesh-quic" --lib: 350 passed.
New in core: reverse egress carries client flows through the dialer
(3 MiB end to end), Require fails closed, validate_egress denial, private
targets refused / LAN opt-in, per-client exit policy (local / pinned to a
label / refused), egress flow limit.

